<Article title="Visible core experience" description="Designing across growth, activation, and member experience at Visible / Verizon." canonicalPath="/case-studies/visible-core-experience.html" eyebrow="From the archive · 2018–2021">
<p class="archive-note">A condensed account from my previous portfolio, retained at its original address. The expanded case study is taking shape.</p>
<h2>My role</h2>
<p>I led design on the Growth and Member Experience teams, working with product managers and engineering leads. I also worked across the Visible design team, Verizon Design Ops, and user research to mentor designers and establish shared practices.</p>
<h2>The challenge</h2>
<p>Visible brought the experience of choosing, activating, and managing phone service into a digital product. As the service grew, the challenge was to keep that journey understandable across the website and mobile apps.</p>
<h2>A connected experience</h2>
<p>My work connected the shopping journey with onboarding, activation, and ongoing membership. The initiatives included:</p>
<ul><li>End-to-end ecommerce across desktop and mobile web.</li><li>Onboarding and activation flows for iOS and Android.</li><li><a href="/case-studies/visible-esim.html">eSIM activation</a>, bringing setup into the app.</li><li>Apple Watch activation and the Swap feature.</li><li>Party Pay and member experience.</li></ul>
<h2>Research and iteration</h2>
<p>I combined primary research with Verizon’s secondary research to identify opportunities. Qualitative findings and behavioral data helped the team examine where the journey was difficult, test changes, and connect customer needs with product decisions.</p>
<h2>Shared design foundations</h2>
<p>Recurring interface patterns became part of a shared design system. Design and engineering review helped those patterns evolve across the product, with accessibility considered as the system grew.</p>
<h2>What stayed with me</h2>
<p>Designing a wireless service means balancing customer needs, business rules, and industry constraints. Keeping product, engineering, brand, and marketing aligned was part of the design work.</p>
